{"id":"3da93f6b-ecd4-4462-b461-da8be804920d","object":"chat.completion","created":1785029796,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dictionary\\\")\\n    if \\\"attempts\\\" not in case or \\\"max_attempts\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must contain 'attempts' and 'max_attempts'\\\")\\n    attempts = case[\\\"attempts\\\"]\\n    max_attempts = case[\\\"max_attempts\\\"]\\n    if not isinstance(attempts, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"attempts must be a list\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(max_attempts, int) or max_attempts <= 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"max_attempts must be a positive integer\\\")\\n    for i, attempt in enumerate(attempts):\\n        if not isinstance(attempt, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"attempt {i} is not a dict\\\")\\n        for key in (\\\"success\\\", \\\"failure_signature\\\", \\\"reflection\\\"):\\n            if key not in attempt:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"attempt {i} missing key '{key}'\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(attempt[\\\"success\\\"], bool):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"attempt {i} success must be bool\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(attempt[\\\"failure_signature\\\"], str):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"attempt {i} failure_signature must be str\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(attempt[\\\"reflection\\\"], str):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"attempt {i} reflection must be str\\\")\\n    n = len(attempts)\\n    if n == 0:\\n        return {\\n            \\\"decision\\\": \\\"retry\\\",\\n            \\\"memory_append\\\": None,\\n            \\\"next_attempt\\\": 1\\n        }\\n    last = attempts[-1]\\n    if last[\\\"success\\\"]:\\n        return {\\n            \\\"decision\\\": \\\"stop_success\\\",\\n            \\\"memory_append\\\": None,\\n            \\\"next_attempt\\\": n\\n        }\\n    if n >= max_attempts:\\n        return {\\n            \\\"decision\\\": \\\"stop_budget\\\",\\n            \\\"memory_append\\\": None,\\n            \\\"next_attempt\\\": n\\n        }\\n    last_sig = last[\\\"failure_signature\\\"]\\n    previous_failure_sigs = set()\\n    for prev in attempts[:-1]:\\n        if not prev[\\\"success\\\"]:\\n            previous_failure_sigs.add(prev[\\\"failure_signature\\\"])\\n    if last_sig in previous_failure_sigs:\\n        return {\\n            \\\"decision\\\": \\\"stop_repeat\\\",\\n            \\\"memory_append\\\": None,\\n            \\\"next_attempt\\\": n\\n        }\\n    reflection = last[\\\"reflection\\\"]\\n    if not reflection:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"reflection is empty, cannot append\\\")\\n    return {\\n        \\\"decision\\\": \\\"retry\\\",\\n        \\\"memory_append\\\": {\\n            \\\"reflection\\\": reflection,\\n            \\\"signature\\\": last[\\\"failure_signature\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"next_attempt\\\": n + 1\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python function solve(case) that processes a case payload. The payload has \"attempts\" (list of objects with success bool, failure_signature string, reflection string), \"max_attempts\" integer, and \"_case_nonce\" string. The function must return a dict with keys \"decision\", \"memory_append\", \"next_attempt\". According to schema, decision is a string, memory_append can be null or object with reflection and signature strings, next_attempt integer.\n\nBased on description: \"With no prior attempt request attempt one.\" So if attempts list is empty, we need to request attempt 1? But the payload always has attempts? The first fixture has attempts with one success. So we need to interpret.\n\nThe mechanism: drive trial loop. They say: \"Stop immediately after success.\" So if last attempt is success, decision should be \"stop_success\"? Or \"stop\"? Need to output decision string. The schema doesn't restrict values, but we need to infer from typical agent control: decisions like \"retry\", \"stop_success\", \"stop_failure\"? Or \"continue\"? The instructions: \"Stop immediately after success.\" So if success, decision \"stop_success\". \"Stop on the registered attempt budget or a repeated failure signature.\" So max_attempts budget, or repeat signature. Others: \"Otherwise request exactly the next attempt.\" So decision \"retry\"? Or \"continue\"? Let's read exact wording: \"Return one JSON object ... The function must return a JSON-serializable object.\" The output schema has decision string. From task description: \"With no prior attempt request attempt one. Stop immediately after success. Stop on the registered attempt budget or a repeated failure signature. Otherwise request exactly the next attempt. Append a nonempty reflection with its signature and return decision, next index, and memory update.\"\n\nSo logic: check attempts list. If no attempts: decision \"request_attempt\" (or \"retry\"?), next_attempt = 1 (since first attempt is 1? But attempt index? They have attempt 0-index? In examples, attempts list indices correspond to attempt numbers? First element is attempt 0? But they say \"request attempt one\". So attempt number 1 is the first attempt. So if no attempts, next_attempt=1. Memory_append should be null because no reflection to append? They say \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature and return decision, next index, and memory update.\" That suggests that on retry, we append memory. But if no prior attempt, no reflection? The instruction: \"With no prior attempt request attempt one.\" That implies we just start, no memory append? But memory_append must be null or object. Probably null when no reflection to append.\n\nNow, if there are attempts, we need to process the last attempt. Check if last attempt success: if yes, decision \"stop_success\", next_attempt? Probably same index? But they said \"Stop immediately after success.\" So next_attempt could be the same attempt number? Or maybe we return the current attempt index? \"next_attempt\" is integer. For success, what should next_attempt be? Since we stop, maybe next_attempt is the index of the successful attempt? Or perhaps it's the next attempt number that would have been tried if not stopped? But they said \"Return decision, next index, and memory update.\" So next_index is presumably the next attempt number to execute. For stop, it might be the current attempt number (no next). But schema requires integer, so we need a value. Possibly we return the current attempt index (0-based? 1-based?). They said \"request attempt one\" for first. So attempt indexing likely 1-based. In the payload attempts list, the first element corresponds to attempt number? The fixtures show attempt objects with success, failure_signature, reflection. They don't have attempt number key. So we need to compute based on list length. If no attempts, next_attempt=1. If we have attempts, and last is success, we stop: decision \"stop_success\". What is next_attempt? Could be len(attempts) (since next attempt after successful one would be that number? Actually if successful, no next, maybe we set next_attempt to the current attempt number? To be consistent, maybe we set next_attempt = len(attempts) (1-indexed) for success? Or 0? Let's think.\n\nConsider fixture case 0: attempts = [ success true, ...]. That is one successful attempt. According to rules: stop after success. So decision \"stop_success\". Memory_append? The last attempt has reflection \"adjust-0\". Should we append that reflection? The instruction: \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature ...\". That suggests that when we do a retry (i.e., not stop) we append memory. For stop, do we append? Possibly not, because we stop immediately after success, so no further memory needed? But the successful attempt already has a reflection; maybe we don't need to append. The output schema requires memory_append either null or object. For stop, likely null. Next_attempt? For stop, maybe the next attempt number could be len(attempts) (which is 1) or 0? In case 0, expected output? Not given. We need to infer from the problem. The task says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`. Its value must be Python source defining `solve(case)`.\" We are not given expected outputs. We need to implement according to spec.\n\nLet's parse the description more: \"Drive a bounded trial loop through act, evaluate, reflect, retry, and terminate states from supplied agent outputs and feedback signals.\" \n\"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: Reflexion control flow and state isolation; base-agent and evaluator model calls are supplied as deterministic fixtures.\"\n\"With no prior attempt request attempt one.\"\n\"Stop immediately after success.\"\n\"Stop on the registered attempt budget or a repeated failure signature.\"\n\"Otherwise request exactly the next attempt.\"\n\"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature and return decision, next index, and memory update.\"\n\nSo the function receives the current state (attempts list and max_attempts). It must decide what to do next. The output is: decision string (e.g., \"retry\", \"stop_success\", \"stop_failure\", \"stop_budget\", \"stop_repeat\"?), memory_append (the reflection from the last attempt if we are retrying? Or if we stop, maybe not), next_attempt (the next attempt number to execute, 1-indexed). The memory_append should be the reflection from the current attempt that we are processing? Or from the last attempt? They say \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature\". That likely means when we decide to retry, we append the reflection of the last attempt (which produced that reflection). For stop, no append.\n\nAlso, they mention \"registered properties: registered_state_machine, reflection_after_feedback, budget_accounting, success_terminates.\" These seem like constraints.\n\nLet's design logic:\n\n- case has \"attempts\" list, \"max_attempts\" int.\n- Determine last attempt index (0-based). Let n = len(attempts). If n == 0: decision \"retry\" (or \"request_attempt\"?), next_attempt = 1, memory_append = null.\n- Else if n > 0:\n   last = attempts[-1]\n   If last[\"success\"] == True: decision \"stop_success\", next_attempt = ? Could be n (the attempt number that succeeded) or maybe n+1? Since we stop, perhaps we set next_attempt = n (the number of attempts made so far)? But they want next index to execute. If stop, no next. Perhaps set to n (the last attempt number). But then caller might interpret as continue? Better to set to 0 or -1? But schema requires integer. Could set to n (current count). In typical retry logic, when stop, next_attempt might be set to the same attempt number to indicate no progress? Not clear.\n   However, consider the phrase \"Return decision, next index, and memory update.\" For success, next index could be the index of the successful attempt? Or maybe it's the next attempt number that would have been tried if not stopped (i.e., current n+1). But since we stop, it's irrelevant. Maybe they expect next_attempt = len(attempts) (current number of attempts) for stop? Let's see fixture case 0: attempts length 1, success. If we set next_attempt=1, then later if we call solve again with that state? But the function is called per fixture row, not in a loop. So the output is the final decision for that state. So next_attempt is part of output, but it's used for logging? Not needed. It must be an integer. I'd guess it should be the number of attempts that have been made (i.e., len(attempts)) when stopping, to indicate that no more attempts are needed. Or maybe it's the next attempt number that would be requested if decision were \"retry\". For stop, it's not used but needs a value. Let's look at the third fixture: attempts length 4, max_attempts 4, all failures, last failure signature e0 which repeats earlier? The earlier signature e0 appears in first and fourth attempts. That constitutes repeated failure signature? The fourth attempt failure signature \"e0\" repeats the first attempt's signature \"e0\". So we should stop on repeated failure signature. Decision \"stop_repeat\"? Or \"stop_failure\"? And next_attempt? So we need consistent rule.\n\nMaybe the expected outputs are standard: decision can be \"stop_success\", \"stop_budget\", \"stop_repeat\", \"retry\". And next_attempt is the attempt number for the next retry (if retry) or the current attempt number if stop. Memory_append is either null (stop) or the reflection from the last attempt (if retry). The reflection is from the last attempt's \"reflection\" field. They said \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature\". So for retry, memory_append = {\"reflection\": last[\"reflection\"], \"signature\": last[\"failure_signature\"]}? But note the last attempt may have success true, but then we stop, not retry. So only for retry.\n\nAlso, check budget: if len(attempts) >= max_attempts, then stop immediately (budget exhausted), even if last might be success? But success terminates earlier, so budget only applies when no success and attempts count equals max_attempts. So if len(attempts) == max_attempts and last success false, then stop on budget. Decision \"stop_budget\". Next_attempt = max_attempts? Or len(attempts) = max_attempts.\n\nRepeated failure signature: check if the last attempt's failure_signature is present in any earlier attempt (excluding last) and that earlier attempt had success false? The spec: \"Stop on the registered attempt budget or a repeated failure signature.\" So if the last attempt's signature matches any previous attempt's failure_signature (regardless of success of previous? Probably yes, but if previous was success, the failure signature field would still be present? In fixtures, success attempts also have failure_signature? The first fixture has success true but also failure_signature \"e0\". That seems odd: